The PCM chunking and the browser capture flow are derived from the public
repository sprechen-io/sprechen-io, commit
1d603f3a75f408d1d11010cd49190e110979803b, which is also MIT,
Copyright (c) 2026 sprechen.io. The derived parts are the signed 16-bit
little-endian mono contract, the getUserMedia error classes, and the
AudioWorklet resampling in
src/MicroSharp.Browser/wwwroot/pcm-recorder-processor.js.
Speech-service credentials, application policies, and study artifacts were
not copied.

OpenAL capture uses the NuGet package OpenTK.Audio.OpenAL 4.9.4, MIT,
https://github.com/opentk/opentk. That package does not contain the native
OpenAL library. On Linux the binding loads libopenal.so.1. The OpenAL Soft
build installed by a distribution is LGPL-2+ and stays outside this package.
Windows CI downloads OpenAL Soft 1.25.2 Win64 from the upstream release
archive and loads it as openal32.dll for the test host only. The archive
COPYING file is the GNU Library General Public License, Version 2. That
binary is not part of this package. This notice is not legal advice.

The bounded PCM history ring and the PCM16LE duration and mean-absolute-amplitude
measurement in MicroSharp.Processing are derived from that same commit.
Speech segmentation, thresholds, text-to-speech, wake words and dialog policy
were not copied. Queue measurements in benchmarks/MicroSharp.Benchmarks time
this library's bounded PCM queue and that history ring on CoreCLR. They are
not browser or WebAssembly measurements.

The name MicroSharp is also used by the public repository
AnmiTaliDev/microsharp, a C# microcontroller compiler. That project is a
different work. No trademark clearance was performed.
